# Copy to ~/.config/paseo/daemon.env (outside the repo — never committed) and
# fill in this machine's values. install.sh copies it there at mode 600 the
# first time only, and config/paseo/paseo.service reads it as an
# EnvironmentFile, so it is how the systemd-supervised daemon on a Linux box
# gets its per-machine settings.
#
# chmod 600 ~/.config/paseo/daemon.env — PASEO_PASSWORD is plaintext.
#
# This is a systemd EnvironmentFile, not a shell script: `KEY=value`, no
# `export`, no command substitution, and quotes are taken literally unless they
# wrap the whole value. Restart after editing:
#
#   systemctl --user restart paseo
#
# Everything here has a counterpart in ~/.paseo/config.json, and env wins over
# that file (precedence is defaults < config.json < env < CLI flags). The
# machine-varying settings live here on purpose: config/paseo/config.json is
# one tracked file shared by every machine, so anything that has to differ
# between the Mac and this box can't live in it.

# ── Listen address ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The daemon binds 127.0.0.1:6767 by default, which no other machine can reach.
# To let the Mac's Paseo app connect, bind this box's *Tailscale* address —
# `tailscale ip -4` — and not 0.0.0.0, which would also publish the daemon on
# every LAN and public Wi-Fi interface the box ever joins.
# PASEO_LISTEN='100.x.y.z:6767'
#
# `listen` is one address, not a list, so binding the tailnet address means the
# daemon is no longer on loopback — and this box's *own* `paseo` CLI defaults to
# localhost:6767 and starts failing with "Cannot connect to daemon". Export
# PASEO_HOST here too (see zshrc.local.example) so the local CLI points at the
# same address the app uses.

# ── Host header allowlist ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set this whenever PASEO_LISTEN is set. It reads like an edge case and isn't:
# the Paseo app connects using whatever name you typed into "Add host", which
# is normally the MagicDNS name, and the daemon rejects any Host header it
# doesn't recognise with `{"error":"Invalid Host header"}` and a
# "Rejected connection from disallowed host" line in the log. Only bare IP
# literals and localhost are accepted unconditionally.
#
# The app sends the *short* name — `myhost:6767`, not the FQDN — so a bare
# tailnet suffix is not enough on its own. A leading dot is a suffix match (the
# daemon tests `host === base || host.endsWith('.' + base)`), and `myhost` does
# not end in `.your-tailnet.ts.net`. List both: the short name for what the app
# actually sends, the suffix for the FQDN form and any other host in the
# tailnet.
#
# Comma-separated, and it appends to rather than replaces the built-in
# localhost entries. `true` means "accept any Host", which is only reasonable
# with a password set below.
# PASEO_HOSTNAMES='myhost,.your-tailnet.ts.net'

# ── Password ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Tailscale encrypts the transport and its ACLs gate who can route to this box,
# but Paseo itself does no authentication otherwise: anything that can reach
# the listen address above can drive every agent on this machine. Set this the
# moment PASEO_LISTEN stops being loopback.
#
# Supply it here as plaintext rather than running `paseo daemon set-password`:
# that command writes a bcrypt hash into ~/.paseo/config.json, and the whole
# point of this file is keeping the secret out of a file install.sh merges from
# the repo. The daemon hashes this value in memory at startup.
# PASEO_PASSWORD='...'

# ── Relay ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Off in config/paseo/config.json, because a direct Tailscale connection needs
# no relay. Turn it on only to reach this box from outside the tailnet.
#
# Careful: setting this pins the value as a launch override, and Paseo then
# *refuses* to let the app change relay at runtime until the override is gone.
# PASEO_RELAY_ENABLED=true

# ── Model API keys ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Unlike everything above, these have no counterpart in ~/.paseo/config.json —
# Paseo never reads them. They are here purely because the daemon's environment
# is what the agent CLIs it launches inherit, and systemd does not source
# ~/.zshrc. The keys in zshrc.local.example reach an interactive shell and stop
# there, so a daemon-launched agent starts with no ANTHROPIC_API_KEY at all.
#
# Leaving them unset is not benign, and the failure is silent and expensive to
# diagnose. omp resolves a provider key by walking: --api-key, models.yml,
# stored OAuth, a /login-saved key, then environment variables — and that last
# step includes `<cwd>/.env`. An already-set process variable beats every .env
# file, which is why omp in a terminal never descends that far. Launched by the
# daemon with the variable absent, omp reaches the *repo's* .env and uses
# whatever it finds. A work checkout that ships a tracked .env full of
# `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=<get this from 1password>` placeholders therefore sends the
# placeholder verbatim, earns a 401 invalid x-api-key, and retry.fallbackChains
# quietly moves the session onto a different provider. The symptom is "only the
# Cursor models work in Paseo", which points nowhere near the cause.
#
# Same values as ~/.zshrc.local (see zshrc.local.example) — keep the two in
# sync. Note the EnvironmentFile syntax rules at the top of this file: no
# `export`, and no command substitution, so `$(op read ...)` will not work here.
#
# Check the other agent CLIs before assuming these are free. An API key in the
# environment can change which credential a CLI picks: Claude Code prefers a
# subscription token in ~/.claude/.credentials.json and prompts once per unknown
# key (recording the answer in customApiKeyResponses in ~/.claude.json), and
# Codex ignores OPENAI_API_KEY while ~/.codex/auth.json says auth_mode=chatgpt.
# Both are fine on a box that already authenticates by API key; on one signed in
# to a subscription, setting these can silently move spend to metered billing.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY='sk-ant-...'
# OPENAI_API_KEY='sk-proj-...'
